  "content": "# Why Python Functions Should Avoid Mutable Default Arguments\n\n## The Core Problem\n\nIn Python, **default argument values are evaluated only once** \u2014 at function definition time, not at each function call. This creates a subtle but dangerous bug when the default value is a **mutable object** (like a list, dictionary, or set).\n\n## Demonstration\n\n```python\n# \u274c BAD: Mutable default argument\ndef append_to_item(item, lst=[]):\n    lst.append(item)\n    return lst\n\nprint(append_to_item(1))  # [1]\nprint(append_to_item(2))  # [",
